### PARKED HERE 2026-09-03 (robert (23)): THE BITSTREAM FAMILY — J2's salvage, "just in case", NOT a task
J2 (the two-layer HEVC file) is DELETED from Phase 1. Robert killed the whole family in one sentence — "if you cant make background frame then i dont need your fucking shit with camera moving blocks" — and he is right: a frame preset SCALES the screen, so every pixel lands somewhere new and no block-reuse scheme of any kind can touch it. A design that leaves the most common edit at full cost does not earn months. NOBODY BUILDS ANY OF THIS WITHOUT ROBERT SAYING SO. What is worth keeping is the research, so it is never re-walked:
- ALREADY RESEARCHED AND DEAD, do not re-derive: block-level patching inside an existing H.264 stream (contamination spreads under motion — a block outside the patched area whose motion vector points into it inherits the change; no exact boundary without our own encoder) · H.264 hidden pictures (none exist: no `pic_output_flag`; `no_output_of_prior_pics_flag` empties the DPB; MVC/SVC base views are what players show) · a second full composite encoder at max (555 Mpx/s wanted against ~416 measured — session.ts:1231 does this arithmetic in place) · a 64 px or 4 px camera grid (robert (22): "not pixel perfect").
- CORRECT AND WORTH KEEPING AS A FACT, independent of J2: THE EVEN-PIXEL GRID. 4:2:0 carries one colour per 2×2 pixels, so a chroma motion vector is the luma one halved — an odd luma offset is a half-pixel chroma offset, which the decoder FILTERS instead of copying. Any exact copy therefore lands on even pixels. This binds the camera drag's snap (under one stage pixel at editor scale) and anything that ever composites by copying. It is the format's limit, not a product compromise.
- HEVC hidden pictures ARE real (`pic_output_flag=0`, in the conformance suite), and the cheap way to find out whether real players honour them is ONE DAY of work with no writer: take a normal HEVC file, rewrite the slice headers of alternate pictures to `pic_output_flag=0`, and play it in QuickTime, Safari, Chrome, VLC, an iPhone, a Windows box. If that census is ever wanted, it is step ZERO — J2 had it as step SIX, after ~6 weeks of writing, which is the wrong order and worth remembering as a planning lesson.
- THE THREE-KIND RULE, kept as discipline and not as machinery: every edit is copies of pixels that exist · a small patch of new pixels · new pixels across the frame. It is the right way to say what an edit COSTS before it runs, and J1's chunk invalidation is the same idea at the granularity this product can actually build.
