WHAT IS BROKEN TODAY (measured 2026-09-02, `exp nativerender`): the render sustains 122-138 fps of 1920x1246 from a 3024x1964@60 source, so a 124-minute take is ~27 min at 30 fps out and ~57 min at 60. That part is honest work. The waste is that it is thrown away: the at-stop pre-render renders the WHOLE take, and `editBindsPrerender` cancels it on any edit and `startPrerender` begins again from zero 1.2 s later — a frame preset and a zoom can cost two discarded hour-long renders before Export is even pressed, on a machine that is then hot for the render that counts. RULED AGAIN 2026-09-03, robert (23), and it narrows this task: the speculation is DELETED, not paced (J3) — chunks are made when EXPORT IS PRESSED, and this is the task Robert named as "small changes with small duration way to be found to apply this changes only on part of video it applies to". No feature is removed and no default moves to get it.
THE SHAPE. The output timeline is cut into GOP-aligned CHUNKS (the export keyframe interval, 5 s). Each chunk renders to its own fmp4 in OPFS keyed by (recording, settings, THE EDIT RESTRICTED TO THAT CHUNK'S OUTPUT SPAN). An export concatenates chunks by PACKET COPY — every chunk shares one encoder config, which is exactly what smart cut already does and byte-compares (`avcC`). An edit then invalidates only the chunks whose pixels actually moved: a zoom keyframe at 40:00 invalidates the seconds it covers and nothing else; a background frame change honestly invalidates all of them, because it changes every pixel — and that is a fact, not a guess. `firstEditDivergenceMs` already exists and is the wrong granularity for this: it answers "where do two edits first differ", and the key answers "does this chunk differ", which is the question that makes an edit cheap.
FALLS OUT OF IT, and is why this absorbs the old J1: the chunk cursor IS the resume cursor (a killed tab resumes at the last complete chunk) and IS the shipping cursor an uploader reads for multi-device and instant-link.
