- `metadata().captureTime` DOES NOT EXIST HERE, measured on a REAL capturer and not inferred. `VideoFrame.metadata()` IS a function and returns `{}` — zero keys — on a real `web-contents-media-stream` display frame. `AudioData` has no `metadata()` at all. Idea 4a's literal field is empty; nothing can be built on it.
- `AudioData.timestamp` IS the anchor, on `performance.now()` DIRECTLY (no epoch conversion), and it does not drift: tab audio 20.61 ms sd 0.76 drift -0.11 ms/min; mic 41.73 ms sd 0.63 drift -0.07 ms/min. Within a take the offset is steady to under 1 ms — far under G5's 6.5 ms per-run term.
- THE FINDING THAT MATTERS MOST, and it is B13's too: **the platform's reported latency is a constant and the real delay is not.** Chrome reports 10.0 ms (2.9 ms at 44.1 kHz) and never varies; the measured capture-to-arrival delay came back 11.7 / 12.9 / 13.5 / 41.6 / 41.7 ms on the mic and 20.6 / 20.8 / 21.0 ms on tab audio across runs. The anchor subtracts the constant. **On every source measured the subtraction is SHORT — mic by 31.6 ms, tab audio by 17.7 ms (raw rung) or 10.8 ms (floor rung) — so the sound is placed that much LATE, never early.**
- `VideoFrame.timestamp` is present on the SYSTEM MONOTONIC clock, constant offset, sd 3.27-6.43 ms and drift -0.74 to +2.65 ms/min across runs on a TAB capturer (damage-driven, so its frame timing is its own subject). A constant offset is a calibration, not a disqualification; the first cut of this probe's verdict rule called it drift and the measurement corrected it. A monitor share may be steadier and is not measured.
- SUBSTITUTED DEVICES READ 0.74 ms for the same audio measurement. The B7 lesson in one line: a synthetic rig would have called this anchor already perfect.
