### R1 the render is too slow — THE ENCODER IS THE WALL; the one untested lever is MORE THAN ONE ENCODER [medium · rig first]
ANSWERED FOR SPEED, REOPENED FOR THROUGHPUT (2026-09-03, robert (23): "now when we out of tricks, we need find ways to make render on export fucking faster"). `exp nativerender` on a 60 s 3024x1964@60 source at the 1080p step, production `exportRecording`, this machine: 122-138 fps sustained, so a 124-minute take is ~27 min at 30 fps out and ~57 min at 60 fps — the hour is the FRAME COUNT.
THE NUMBER EVERYTHING NOW HANGS ON, already measured and under-used: ON A NATIVE-RESOLUTION SOURCE THE ENCODER IS 83-96 % OF THE LOOP'S WALL CLOCK (qp20: 62.1 s wall, 51.4 s encode-wait, 58 fps · bitrate target: 39.7 s wall, 38.0 s encode-wait, 91 fps — scripts/encode-cost.mjs's header). Decode, draw, mux and audio all live INSIDE that wait. So "make the render faster" is exactly and only "get more out of the encoder", and any proposal that does not touch the encoder is arguing over 4-17 % before it starts.
THIS DELETES ITS OWN OLD LEVER, and the arithmetic is the whole argument: overlapping decode and encode was written here at ~1.4×. It cannot be. If encode-wait is 83 % the ceiling from removing ALL decode time is 1.20×; at 96 % it is 1.04×. And X4 already measured the shape (decode farm 0.99-1.08× against a >=2x gate) because DECODE AND ENCODE CONTEND FOR ONE MEDIA ENGINE — the same decode takes 4.7 s alone and 17-23 s beside the encoder — while O5b says the export is already pipelined. A second JS thread does not create a second engine. The lever is closed; do not spend a session on it.
THE TWO NUMBERS NOBODY HAS, and the rig for both already exists:
- (1) WHAT THIS ENCODER SUSTAINS AT MAX. `scripts/encode-cost.mjs` takes `--width/--height` and has NEVER been run at 3024x1964 (BACKLOG P1 asks for exactly this, idle machine, 60 fps duty cycle). Every encoder-budget argument on this roadmap — B14's, M1's, PROPOSALS A's +5 % — is currently an argument about a number nobody has. rate.ts says 416 Mpx/s, this rig says 120-188 through a real render, Robert's own take ran at 309. They disagree 3.5x.
- (2) WHETHER MORE THAN ONE ENCODER AT A TIME RAISES THE TOTAL. The rig benches ONE VideoEncoder across hardware preference, quantizer vs bitrate, latency mode and frames-in-flight. It has never run TWO, or four. One session submits a frame and waits for a driver round trip; the engine may well be idle between frames, in which case N sessions raise aggregate throughput on the SAME engine. If aggregate is flat, the engine is saturated and the render's ceiling is proven — which is worth knowing for good. ~20 lines on the rig (`--parallel=N`), one headed run, one hour.
WHY (2) IS THE WHOLE PRIZE: if aggregate rises, J1 delivers it for free. J1's chunks are disjoint output spans that already share ONE encoder config and concatenate by PACKET COPY (smart cut does this today and byte-compares `avcC`), so N workers each with their own decoder+encoder render N chunks at once. A 2x on the engine is a 2x on every render this product will ever do, at no cost to picture, file or feature.
